      "content": "I see. So it's less about actual crime and more about a psychological shift in how the neighborhood feels. You mentioned these \"anxieties\" and the shift away from \"homogenous norms.\" \n\nCould you go a bit deeper into the sociological theory behind why these specific groups—South Asian or Middle Eastern—are often perceived as being in more direct conflict with those white, middle-class norms? Is there something about their specific cultural or social structures that makes them inherently more disruptive to the \"order\" or \"stability\" found in these Western suburban settings compared to, say, other immigrant groups? I want to understand if there's a fundamental incompatibility being discussed in policy circles.",
